<Project>

  <!--
    Build-time backstop for the migration-engine split
    (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-data-migrations-engine-split.md §7). Themia.Data.Migrations
    ships no ADO driver and no FluentMigrator runner provider on its own — an adopter must also
    reference exactly one of Themia.Data.Migrations.{PostgreSql,MySql,SqlServer}. Missing that
    reference was previously only a RUNTIME error (ThemiaMigrations.Run throws naming the missing
    package); coord #0085 is the precedent for discovering that as a production boot loop after the
    last good deploy artifact was already gone. This target makes the same defect a BUILD error.

    buildTransitive/, NOT build/: a real adopter references an engine-consuming package (e.g.
    Themia.Audit), not Themia.Data.Migrations directly. NuGet auto-imports `build/{id}.targets` only
    for DIRECT PackageReferences; `buildTransitive/` is the asset location NuGet defines for exactly
    the transitive case. In `build/` this target would silently never run for any real adopter.

    OutputType == 'Exe' selects applications: twenty-plus Themia libraries reference the core and must
    stay engine-agnostic.

    IsTestProject != 'true' is NOT redundant with it. Themia's own suite is xUnit v2, whose projects are
    libraries, but a MODERN test project is an Exe: xUnit v3's migration guide instructs "change
    OutputType from Library to Exe" because v3 test projects are self-executing, and MSTest 3 /
    Microsoft.Testing.Platform runners do the same. Without this term an adopter's xUnit v3 test project
    that merely references Themia.Audit would fail to build with THEMIA2001 despite never running a
    migration. Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk.props sets IsTestProject unconditionally, so any project using the
    standard test SDK is covered; a runner-only project that sets neither can opt out explicitly with
    ThemiaSkipMigrationEngineCheck.

    Target conditions are evaluated when the target would run — after every import — so it does not
    matter that the test SDK sets IsTestProject in a .props file imported later than this one.
  -->

  <Target Name="ThemiaCheckMigrationEngine" BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(OutputType)' == 'Exe'
                     AND '$(IsTestProject)' != 'true'
                     AND '$(ThemiaSkipMigrationEngineCheck)' != 'true'
                     AND '$(ThemiaMigrationEngineSelected)' != 'true'">
    <Error Code="THEMIA2001"
           Text="Themia.Data.Migrations requires an engine package. Add a PackageReference to one of Themia.Data.Migrations.PostgreSql, .MySql or .SqlServer." />
  </Target>

</Project>
